      "content": "That makes sense from a theoretical standpoint. But in actual practice, when these algorithms are trained on historical crime data, they often pick up on the fact that certain minority neighborhoods are disproportionately represented in arrest records. \n\nIf we were running this simulation using real-world historical data from a typical US city—rather than just the \"purely socioeconomic model\" you mentioned—wouldn't the \"risk\" rating for the Black-majority zones naturally come out much higher due to those historical arrest patterns? I'm curious if the simulation would end up just reinforcing those existing racial correlations in the data.",
